  "id": "DrugBank_DB02659 - MONDO_12672",
  "verbalization": "Cholic acid is implicated in the development of cholelithiasis through a series of indirect but interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that cholic acid is associated with cholelithiasis via the intermediate of chenodeoxycholic acid, which is linked to both choledocholithiasis and cholecystolithiasis. This indicates a potential role of cholic acid in the formation of gallstones, likely through its ability to alter bile composition and promote stone formation. The presence of multiple pathways, including those involving ursodeoxycholic acid, highlights the complexity of the relationship and suggests that cholic acid may influence the disease process through different mechanisms. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of bile acid metabolism in the pathogenesis of cholelithiasis, with cholic acid potentially acting as a precursor or modulator in this process.",
